      "id": 1,
      "level": "beginner",
      "q": "Practice: static todo UI on S3 + HTTPS",
      "a": "Summary\nSummary: static todo UI on S3 + HTTPS. Bucket for files. CloudFront + ACM for https://. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Walk the snippet like this: upload dist/ to S3. CloudFront default root index.html. In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. A restart does not wipe the only disk you had. Keys stay in a secret store. Interviewers want IAM vs app roles named separately. Do not commit keys. 0.0.0.0/0 on SSH is an open door. IAM is not the same as isAdmin in your JWT. S3 is not a SQL database.\n\nThe problem before\nFiles sat on one laptop. Keys were in Git. SSH was open to the world. The UI was http. Nobody knew who paid when a script looped. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. Bucket for files. CloudFront + ACM for https://. Do not commit keys. 0.0.0.0/0 on SSH is an open door. IAM is not the same as isAdmin in your JWT. S3 is not a SQL database.\n\nWhat this is\nBucket for files. CloudFront + ACM for https://. Cloud labs put the UI on S3+HTTPS, compute on EC2 or Lambda, data on RDS, doors in IAM and security groups. The app still does CRUD. The cloud is the building, not a new kind of todo. Walk the snippet like this: upload dist/ to S3. CloudFront default root index.html. A restart does not wipe the only disk you had. Keys stay in a secret store. Interviewers want IAM vs app roles named separately.\n\nWhat it solves\nA restart does not wipe the only disk you had. Keys stay in a secret store. Interviewers want IAM vs app roles named separately. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). Bucket for files. CloudFront + ACM for https://. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nReal-life example\nA rented shop: lockers (S3), a kitchen you do not stand in all day (Lambda), a safe (RDS), name badges (IAM). You still write the menu. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet. static todo UI on S3 + HTTPS. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nUses\nStatic sites, APIs, uploads, alarms. Same HTTPS and auth rules as a VPS. On this sheet, the question is: Practice: static todo UI on S3 + HTTPS. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. A restart does not wipe the only disk you had. Keys stay in a secret store. Interviewers want IAM vs app roles named separately.\n\nWatch out\nDo not commit keys. 0.0.0.0/0 on SSH is an open door. IAM is not the same as isAdmin in your JWT. S3 is not a SQL database. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview.",
